            <ArticleTitle>The effects of single bouts of body-weight supported treadmill training on the feeling states of people with spinal cord injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>112-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pre-test, post-test study of the relationship between exercise-related changes in feeling states and pain among persons with chronic incomplete spinal cord injury (SCI) who performed three separate exercise sessions consisting of body-weight supported treadmill training (BWSTT).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine whether exercise-related changes in feeling states are related to exercise-related changes in pain and in-task pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hamilton, Ontario, Canada.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 14 men and women with chronic, incomplete SCI (ASIA B and C) performed three exercise sessions of BWSTT. Measures of pain and feeling states were administered before and after each session along with a measure of pain experienced while exercising.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Participants who experienced greater decreases in pain from pre- to post-exercise also experienced greater improvements in feeling states. Pain experienced during exercise was unrelated to changes in feeling states.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Among persons with SCI, exercise-related changes in pain can influence the effects of individual exercise bouts on feeling states.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
